__pycache__/
*.pyc
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
data/*.csv
# the whole of data/ is generated or scratch, and one file in it is not mine:
# bugtest.py is Wade's ACRO repro and was swept into a commit by a broad
# `git add` because only the CSVs were ignored. Ignore the directory and keep
# the generator's output, rather than relying on remembering.
data/
redteam/results.csv
redteam/acro_results.csv
redteam/realmodel_results.txt
.venv/
.pytest_cache/
.hypothesis/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
*.egg-info/
dist/
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.db.key
# the audit chain's high-water mark (#75) and the one-process claim (#81) are
# sidecars of a runtime database, not source. Neither is secret — a MAC
# discloses nothing and the claim file holds a pid — but a broad `git add`
# committing an operator's chain head is exactly the shape of #57.
*.db.head
*.db.lock
*.db.head.*.tmp
site/

# `nohup uv run uvicorn ...` is the documented way to start the demo server
# (scripts/restart_web.sh, and the accessibility CI job), so this file appears
# on any machine that has ever run it.
nohup.out

# Retained audit chains from past red-team rounds. NOT reproducible — they are
# historical evidence, kept because re-MACing a polluted chain is precisely the
# operation the design exists to prevent (see the README inside). The `.db` and
# `.db.key` were already covered by extension; the directory and its README
# were not, so they sat in `git status` inviting exactly the broad `git add`
# that caused #57.
.audit-archive/

# binary presentation artifacts are distributed via releases, not git
# (see artifacts/README.md). NOTE: `.ppt` is deliberately NOT ignored — three
# plain-language explainer decks are tracked on purpose, so a blanket rule
# would be wrong. A stray `.ppt` is therefore something to notice in review.
artifacts/*.pptx
artifacts/*.pdf
.DS_Store
.~lock.*#
# LaTeX build artifacts (the preprint PDF is distributed via releases)
paper/*.aux
paper/*.bbl
paper/*.blg
paper/*.log
paper/*.out
paper/*.pdf

# generated deck screenshots
artifacts/shots/

# Lean build artifacts (formal/lean)
formal/lean/.lake/

# The bestiary card art is generated at ~1.5 MB a card (38 MB for the set).
# `docs/figures/bestiary/` holds the committed derivatives — same pixels, same
# dimensions, re-encoded to a 256-colour palette (max mean error 2.4/255), which
# is 9.4 MB instead of 38. Keep the originals here locally; they do not belong
# in the history of a repo that was deliberately slimmed to 3.4 MB.
docs/bestiaryImaging/
